          "redirectURL": "",
          "content": {
            "mimeType": "application/json",
            "text": "{\"events\":[{\"id\":\"6a699667336242e137d8f6c5\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"Quand les étoiles rencontrent la mer + The Green Ray + workshop with Daniel Owusu\",\"description\":\"<p><strong><em>Quand les étoiles rencontrent la mer </em>(1996) dir. Raymond Rajaonarivelo</strong></p><p><br></p><p>Kapila is born on the day of a solar eclipse, a visible disharmony in the heavens resulting in  children born in such circumstances possessing great and dangerous powers according to local tradition in Madagascar. Losing his mother during childbirth, and abandoned by his father, a young woman with no children saves Kapila, changing his fate for ever and taking him to the capital city where he is confronted with the harsh and violent side of poverty and forges friendships despite his status as a social outcaste. Once he reaches adulthood, Kapila realises that he has secret powers he cannot control and goes on a journey that takes him back to his father and birthplace. <em>Quand les étoiles rencontrent la mer </em>is a mythological fable, inspired by Madagascan legends playing out between the wide expanse of Madagascan countryside and the ramshackle alleys of the capital Antananarivo.</p><p><br></p><p><strong><em>The Green Ray </em>(2001) dir. Tacita Dean</strong></p><p><br></p><p>In this 16mm film shot on the Madagascan coastline, Tacita Dean shows us the green ray, the slowest setting ray of light just before the sun disappears on the horizon. The artist was able to capture this elusive phenomenon using celluloid film, in her conviction that this format offers the possibility to see something that digital cameras cannot. Dean has devoted much of her practice to advocating for the permanence of analog film technology in the future, not instead but alongside digital formats, as the comparison between the images each medium produce offers insights into our perception of the world around us. While the green ray is caused by a combination of the dispersion, scattering, and refraction of light, the film aims to capture a moment of sublime yet fleeting experience.</p><p><br></p><p>Total film runtime: 80\\\"</p><p><br></p><p><strong>The screening will be preceded by an analogue filmmaking activity&nbsp;led by artist Daniel Owusu.</strong> Sharing 100 feet of 16mm film, audience members will have the opportunity to take part in a collaborative portrait session. Working together, the group will light, frame and film one another, creating a series of five-second moving portraits. The exposed film will then be hand-developed and solarised during the screening, being projected at the end of the evening. The finished film will remain as a collective record of friends, collaborators and supporters who took part in Brixton Community Cinema’s programme.</p><p><br></p><p><strong style=\\\"background-color: rgba(209, 73, 0, 0.02); color: rgb(51, 51, 51);\\\">Daniel Owusu</strong><span style=\\\"background-color: rgba(209, 73, 0, 0.02); color: rgb(51, 51, 51);\\\"> is a London-based artist working across drawing, writing, and filmmaking. His practice is concerned with memory, storytelling, and the construction of language, often exploring how narratives are formed, fragmented, and shared across time and communities.&nbsp;</span></p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"MayDay Rooms\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a699aed2cb0183e52c5729a\",\"name\":\"MayDay Rooms\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"88 Fleet St, London EC4Y 1DH, United Kingdom\",\"default_capacity\":40,\"color\":\"#cef2ec\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-08-12T17:00:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-08-12T19:15:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":false,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":35,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a699902ce4beb9cd9eea32f\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":600,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":35,\"quantity_sold\":35,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":0,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":100,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-07-29T16:41:05.894Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785305881530temp-1785305881482-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785305881744temp-1785305881708-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a6c62d319f5d254f9b0299b\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"The Dislocation of Amber & Of Dust and Rubies, a Film on Suspension with the Sudanese Kitchen + discussion with Eiman Hussein\",\"description\":\"<p><strong><em>The Dislocation of Amber</em> (1975) dir. Hussein Shariffe 32\\\"</strong></p><p><br></p><p>Filmed in Suakin, once a thriving Red Sea port and now marked by ruin, <em>The Dislocation of Amber</em> is less an explanatory document than an elegy in cinematic fragments. Sudanese filmmaker, painter, and poet Hussein Shariffe layers architecture, shoreline, sung poetry, and symbolic imagery to evoke a city suspended between opulence and abandonment, trade memory and historical erasure. The film’s title signals an impossible separation— beauty from devastation, life from aftermath—and this tension structures the viewing experience. Rather than narrating decline in linear terms, Shariffe composes a sensory archaeology in which stones, textures, and sounds carry the burden of history.</p><p><br></p><p><strong><em>Of Dust and Rubies: A Film on Suspension </em>(2020) dir. Tamer El Said 49\\\"</strong></p><p><br></p><p>In 2000 Hussein Shariffe began working on his final film <em>Of Dust and Rubies. </em>The filming was done at locations in Egypt, where he had been living in exile for 10 years due to his stance toward the Sudanese regime. His sudden death brought his work to an abrupt end and since then <em>Of Dust and Rubies</em> has remained incomplete, a film in a state of perpetual suspension. In 2018 a group of five individuals came together–including Shariffe’s daughter Eiman Hussein as well as the film’s main actor Tala Afifi–to discuss how the work of this exceptional director could be presented to the world. Among the results of this meeting is the film<strong> </strong><em>Of Dust and Rubies: A Film on Suspension</em> by Tamer El Said.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>Following the screening we will be joined for a discussion by psychotherapist, archivist and writer <strong>Eiman Hussein</strong>, daughter of Hussein Shariffe.</p><p><br></p><p>This event is hosted in collaboration with<strong> the Sudanese Kitchen</strong>, a project dedicated to preserving traditional Sudanese recipes and food culture.</p><p><br></p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"Stockwell Park Community Centre\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a6c60bd3d4a792ee1619cf9\",\"name\":\"Stockwell Park Community Centre\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"21 Aytoun Place, \\nLondon \\nSW9 0TE\\nUnited Kingdom\\n\",\"default_capacity\":100,\"color\":\"#608ea4\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-09-09T18:00:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-09-09T20:00:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":100,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a6c6ae9cff77a501d0cd515\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":600,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":100,\"quantity_sold\":54,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":46,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":100,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-08-10T18:31:28.732Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785490154611temp-1785490154590-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785490154797temp-1785490154792-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a6b1577158c4dd6568e5c70\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"Jago Hua Savera (The Day Shall Dawn) with Other Cinemas\",\"description\":\"<p><strong><em>Jago Hua Savera (The Day Shall Dawn) </em>(1959) dir. </strong><strong style=\\\"color: rgb(31, 31, 31);\\\">A. J. Kardar.</strong></p><p><br></p><p>On the banks of the River Meghna, best friends Mian and Qasim eke out a humble existence as fishermen. Their biggest dream is to own their own boat so that they can work for themselves. When Mian’s wife gives birth and their eldest child falls sick, his sister comes to take care of the family, soon becoming the object of desire of both Qasim and the town’s deceitful loan shark. A death in the village sparks the opportunity for the community to redress status quo in the village, and a battle for justice and dignity ensues in this fatalistic black-and-white tale. Written by renowned Pakistani poet and communist thinker Faiz Ahmad Faiz, <em>Jago Hua Savera</em> is a rare collaboration between artists and intellectuals in India, Pakistan and what would later become Bangladesh, executed through the austere lens of Italian neorealism.</p><p><br></p><p>Screening in collaboration with <strong>Other Cinemas</strong>, an artist-led project that uses film to bring people together to imagine and build just futures for Black and non-white communities.</p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"Other Cinemas\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a6b168a8ddd096dfdb49d47\",\"name\":\"Other Cinemas\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"Metroland Studios, \\n91 Kilburn Square, \\nNW6 6PS\",\"default_capacity\":75,\"color\":\"#8d95ff\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-09-14T17:30:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-09-14T20:00:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":true,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":75,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a6b16816e9fe4b9cdb5acee\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":0,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":75,\"quantity_sold\":6,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":69,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":false,\"variable_price_minimum\":0,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-08-10T18:31:38.833Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785402963782temp-1785402963728-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785402964047temp-1785402964015-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a82d9ddc018fadf2ccc63ba\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"Pakistani Cinema Vinyl Listening Session with Haseeb Iqbal\",\"description\":\"<p>Broadcaster and DJ Haseeb Iqbal presents a very special listening sessions, exploring the stories and sounds of Pakistani cinema records.</p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"Studio 12\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6aa07e506d8505b257b834ea\",\"name\":\"Studio 12\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"1 Addington Square, \\nSE5 7JZ\",\"default_capacity\":40,\"color\":\"#ffbc57\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-09-21T18:00:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-09-21T20:00:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":40,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a82de0d642a9cf4b4c767db\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":600,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":40,\"quantity_sold\":31,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":9,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":600,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-13T20:36:20.025Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a6c5fe31a0308388b33af1c\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"Dhalinyaro with Ifriqiya Cinema\",\"description\":\"<p><strong style=\\\"color: rgb(36, 28, 21);\\\"><em>Dhalinyaro </em>(2017) dir. Lula Ali Ismaïl 86\\\"</strong></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"color: rgb(36, 28, 21);\\\">In Djibouti's coastal capital, three schoolgirls waver between adolescence and womanhood, navigating the rush of dating, pressure of university-entry exams and life-changing consequences that both present. Despite coming from distinctly different households, all find themselves misunderstood by their families, instead finding solace in each other sharing hushed classroom conversations and daydreaming at the beach. The first Djiboutian film directed by a woman, </span><em style=\\\"color: rgb(36, 28, 21);\\\">Dhalinyaro</em><span style=\\\"color: rgb(36, 28, 21);\\\"> is a tender film about the fragile and affirming nature of friendship, deftly portraying its embrace, ruptures, and reconfigurations.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">In collaboration with </span><strong style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Ifriqiya Cinema</strong><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">,</span><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 17, 53);\\\"> a research and film programming collective, set up in 2024 by Amina Ali and Hajar Meddah. Their goal is to share films from across Africa and its diasporas with the hopes of encouraging a broader and more multidimensional understanding of Africa through the medium of film.</span></p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"Stockwell Park Community Centre\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a6c60bd3d4a792ee1619cf9\",\"name\":\"Stockwell Park Community Centre\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"21 Aytoun Place, \\nLondon \\nSW9 0TE\\nUnited Kingdom\\n\",\"default_capacity\":100,\"color\":\"#608ea4\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-09-22T18:00:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-09-22T20:00:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":100,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a6c608adfb6b792e0830c1f\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":600,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":100,\"quantity_sold\":64,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":36,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":100,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-08-10T18:31:58.627Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785487633792temp-1785487633733-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785487634024temp-1785487633980-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a6b17e34b83e756a061599e\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"Kuxa Kanema: The Birth of Cinema & Apostles of Cinema with the June Givanni Pan-African Cinema Archive + discussion\",\"description\":\"<p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">A double bill of films from the Swahili Coast, exploring the trials and tribulations of bringing cinema to the masses.</span></p><p><br></p><p><strong><em>Kuxa Kanema: O Nascimento do Cinema (Kuxa Kanema: The Birth of Cinema)</em> (2003)&nbsp;dir. Margarida Cardoso 53\\\"</strong></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">After 500 years of Portuguese colonial rule, Mozambique was one of the last African countries to gain independence in 1975. The first cultural act of the state under the FRELIMO (Mozambique Liberation Front) government was to create the National Institute of Cinema and through it, launch&nbsp;an educational anti-capitalist newsreel program for the masses. Named </span><em style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Kuxa Kanema</em><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">, it’s mission was ‘to capture the image of the people on film, and deliver it back to the people.’&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Having found the footage and institutions which housed it in ruins, filmmaker Margarida Cardoso uses their deterioration to trace the life and death of a revolutionary government–caught between nation building and the aggressions of the pro-apartheid governments of neighbouring Rhodesia and South Africa–and the extinguished nascent cinema that accompanied it.</span></p><p><br></p><p><strong><em>Apostles of Cinema </em>(2022) dir. Darragh Amelia, Gertrude Malizana, Cece Mlay &amp; Jesse Gerard Mpango 16\\\"</strong></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">In the present day, in Dar es Salaam (where FRELIMO was both founded and offered an operational base) film collective Ajabu Ajabu tour the city’s cinemas. As with Mozambique, institutional cinemas are few and far between but informal and improvised ones abound and offer spaces for communion. Beneath its witty and maximalist veneer,</span><em style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"> Apostles of Cinema</em><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"> foregrounds questions of agency, ownership, access and piracy in cinema, offering a critique and counter-method to the colonial and market logics that dictate film distribution.</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Included in your ticket are snacks by </span><a href=\\\"https://www.instagram.com/sahanisaanii/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"><strong>S</strong></a><a href=\\\"https://www.instagram.com/sahanisaanii/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\"><strong>ahani Saanii</strong></a>, a non-fixed food research and learning space engaging people in culinary histories of Western Indian and the Swahili Coast through suppers, workshops, and other experimental formats.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Following the film, Brixton Community Cinema founder </span><strong style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Abiba Coulibaly</strong><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"> will be in conversation with the multi-generational team behind the </span><strong style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">June Givanni Pan-African Cinema Archive</strong><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"> team for an open discussion on their experiences sharing Pan-African cinema with audiences in a variety of contexts; from community centres to international festivals, across the continent and in the diaspora!</span></p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"color: rgb(26, 27, 31);\\\">The </span><strong style=\\\"color: rgb(26, 27, 31);\\\">June Givanni Pan-African Cinema Archive</strong><span style=\\\"color: rgb(26, 27, 31);\\\"> holds a unique collection of artefacts and archival material, which has at its core the interest of Pan-African cinema and its relationship with Black British cinema and culture.</span></p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"MayDay Rooms\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a699aed2cb0183e52c5729a\",\"name\":\"MayDay Rooms\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"88 Fleet St, London EC4Y 1DH, United Kingdom\",\"default_capacity\":40,\"color\":\"#cef2ec\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-09-23T17:30:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-09-23T19:30:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":40,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a6b660d275c252740404a8e\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":600,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":40,\"quantity_sold\":37,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":3,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":100,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-23T15:52:56.368Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785421019904temp-1785421019844-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785421020251temp-1785421020208-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a6f2a2a23fe561f2f395a7c\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"An Incomplete Calendar + intro by Elhum Shakerifar\",\"description\":\"<p><strong><em>An Incomplete Calendar </em>(2026) dir. Sanaz Sohrabi 77\\\"</strong></p><p><br></p><p><em>﻿An Incomplete Calendar</em> is a story about oil, music, and the Third World project. The film explores the remarkable and untold story of a musical vinyl titled <em>Rhymes and Songs for OPEC</em>, recorded in 1980 by the Concert Choir of Central University of Venezuela to commemorate the 20th anniversary of Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries (OPEC), established in Baghdad in 1960.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>The film interweaves forgotten songs and rarely seen archives to tell a different story of oil, not as a commodity but rather as a political leverage that was used for the liberation struggles in Palestine and building Pan-Arab solidarity, a transnational project that was shattered by the triumph of neoliberal economics in 1970s.</p><p><br></p><p>This screening will be followed by a conversation with <strong style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Elhum Shakerifar,</strong><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"> an award-winning documentary producer and curator with a particular interest in film from the Global South, who sits on the board of the Palestine Film Institute and co-curates the Palestine Film Platform. Elhum is also a poet, translator and behind the London-based company </span><a href=\\\"https://hakawati.co.uk/about/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Hakawati</a><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">.</span></p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"Stockwell Park Community Centre\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a6c60bd3d4a792ee1619cf9\",\"name\":\"Stockwell Park Community Centre\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"21 Aytoun Place, \\nLondon \\nSW9 0TE\\nUnited Kingdom\\n\",\"default_capacity\":100,\"color\":\"#608ea4\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-09-30T18:00:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-09-30T20:00:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":100,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a79f5bf72d5b546f731ced5\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":600,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":100,\"quantity_sold\":26,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":74,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":100,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-16T22:54:05.893Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785670494737temp-1785670494670-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785670494962temp-1785670494911-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a735b62e36ec68bd8b82447\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"Visions from the Underground: British Asian Sonic Culture on Film + discussion\",\"description\":\"<p>Defining the Asian Underground has always proved a tough task. Was it a movement? A genre? A youth culture? A self-defined collective of practitioners and spaces? A nebulous web of overlapping sounds, people, experiences and outlooks? And was it ever really underground?</p><p><br></p><p>This much we do know: during the late 1980s and throughout the 1990s, new sonic forms and club spaces emerged from, and for, South Asian people in Britain. From Club Kali—a queer club night founded by DJ Ritu that originated from the South Asian LGBTQIA+ organisation Shakti—to Talvin Singh’s Anokha—a crucible of ephemeral cool in late 1990s Hoxton where young British South Asians danced alongside a multicultural cohort of East Londoners, as well as the likes of Afrika Bambaataa, Aphex Twin, and Björk—the second generation was appropriating and transforming dominant musical forms, formulating new cultural expressions, and perhaps, making a new claim on British identity itself. Taken as a whole, the scene became known as the Asian Underground.</p><p><br></p><p>This trio of films offers a potted history of that time. We hear from the producers and progenitors of the culture and its spaces, as well as the everyday people who enjoyed these new dis-orienting rhythms and dance floor spatialities. In painting this picture—which is by no means complete—we can also start to understand the Asian Underground from below, reading these films as documentary evidence of a social history that is only beginning to be told.</p><p><br></p><p>•⁠&nbsp;<strong><em>⁠Brimful of Asia</em></strong> (1998) dir. Pratibha Parmar 24\\\"</p><p>•⁠<em>&nbsp;</em><strong><em>⁠Talvin Singh: Sounds of the Asian Underground</em></strong> dir. Tony Knox (1997) 25\\\"</p><p>•⁠<em>&nbsp;</em><strong><em>⁠Identical Beat</em></strong> dir. Smita Malde (1994) 25\\\"</p><p><br></p><p><strong>The screening will start at 6:30PM sharp</strong>, and be followed by a discussion with<strong> Pratibha Parmar, DJ Ritu &amp; Tariq Jazeel,</strong> led by <strong>Kulraj Phullar.</strong></p><p><br></p><p>Event curated by <strong>Julian Ehsan, </strong>an archivist and curator based in London. His work centres on platforming and celebrating family, community, and social histories of the South Asian diaspora.</p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"The Rio Cinema\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a735b160be0d7cf412588ae\",\"name\":\"The Rio Cinema\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"107 Kingsland High Street,\\nDalston,\\nLondon, E8 2PB\",\"default_capacity\":400,\"color\":\"#7fff36\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-10-02T17:30:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-10-02T19:30:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":400,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a735f2cc4d5cdd35c171b65\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":600,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":400,\"quantity_sold\":221,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":179,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":100,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-10-02T16:00:50.747Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785947498369temp-1785947498314-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785947498601temp-1785947498561-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a70c4a87035c813460da4b5\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"⁠⁠In Murky Waters: Sapphics & Spirits of the Indian Ocean with Em—Dash\",\"description\":\"<p>a <strong><u>DOUBLE FEATURE</u></strong> screening presented to you by Em—Dash..</p><p>The magnetism and disquiet of femininity are explored through a queer Malayali drama and an archival Indonesian horror</p><p><br></p><p><strong>˖𓍢ִ໋ꨄ︎&nbsp;Sancharram </strong>(The Journey)</p><p>India, 2004, 107 min. Dir. Ligy J. Pullappally.</p><p><br></p><p><strong>𓐐𓎩 Light Bites </strong>- 30 min snack break!</p><p>Hot chai and homemade snacks from South India &amp; Indonesia will be served</p><p><br></p><p><strong>.ೃ࿔* Bangunnya Nyi Roro Kidul </strong>(The Awakening of the Queen of the Southern Seas)</p><p>Indonesia, 1985, 100 min. Dir. Sisworo Gautama Putra.</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Schedule</strong></p><p>1:30pm: Doors open</p><p>2:15pm: <em>Sancharram</em> plays</p><p>4:15pm: Snack break</p><p>4:40pm: <em>Bangunnya Nyi Roro Kidul </em>plays</p><p>6:40pm: Event end</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong><em>Sancharram </em>(2004), directed by Ligy J. Pullappally</strong></p><p>A Malayalam-language coming-of-age drama that traces the sapphic relationship between two young women, Kiran and Delilah, in rural Kerala. The film explores queer desire as an ordinary and deeply felt form of intimacy. Through its portrayal of everyday life, <em>Sancharram</em> foregrounds the tensions between personal autonomy and social convention, examining how love is negotiated within tightly regulated structures of kinship, religion, and community.</p><p><br></p><p><strong><em>Bangunnya Nyi Roro Kidul</em></strong> <strong>(1985), directed by Sisworo Gautama Putra</strong></p><p>Three college students dive headfirst into the Indian Ocean in hopes of finding Nyi Roro Kidul, the fabled spirit and queen of Java’s Southern Seas. The only thing they could find was the mysterious corpse of a beautiful woman. In this film, legendary actress Suzzanna—the undisputed queen of Indonesian horror—navigates life as a mortal model while mischievously enacting vengeance on men who have no respect for boundaries. For those who can’t do horror, this kitchy film is accented with lo-fi sfx and visual gags that will surely make you giggle. No overly extended suspense or jumpscares, so you're safe! (unless...)</p><p><br></p><p><br></p><p><strong>Em—Dash</strong></p><p>is a South East London based studio + press. We experiment with the book-form, playfully publishing work that spans across art-practice, design, research, and visual culture. Beyond books, we have worked across mediums of curation, film and radio. We also run workshops to share ideas, skills and facilitate collective making.&nbsp;Em—Dash is operated by artists and designers Aarushi Matiyani + Saundra Liemantoro.</p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"Art Hub Studios\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6aa97ea4eafe7df6c5c455f3\",\"name\":\"Art Hub Studios\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"Stanley St, New Cross, London, SE8 4BL, United Kingdom\",\"default_capacity\":null,\"color\":\"#e0b0ff\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-10-03T13:00:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-10-03T18:00:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":99,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a70c57c1915677d499f9c94\",\"name\":\"PWYC (Standard)\",\"price\":892,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":33,\"quantity_sold\":33,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":0,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":false,\"variable_price_minimum\":0,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null},{\"id\":\"6a70c57c1915677d499f9c98\",\"name\":\"PWYC (Supporter)\",\"price\":1375,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":33,\"quantity_sold\":1,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":32,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":false,\"variable_price_minimum\":0,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null},{\"id\":\"6a70c57c1915677d499f9c9c\",\"name\":\"PWYC (Concession)\",\"price\":603,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":33,\"quantity_sold\":9,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":24,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":false,\"variable_price_minimum\":0,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-09-19T23:13:22.816Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1786480698947temp-1786480698935-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1786480699155temp-1786480699133-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]},{\"id\":\"6a6a2d6016bdd62ce0f747e9\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"The Sea Is a Blue Memory shorts programme + ESEA Community Centre Fundraiser\",\"description\":\"<p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Join ESEA Film Night and Brixton Community Cinema for a programme curated by Suyin Haynes, </span>exploring the role of the sea as a keeper of memories,<span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"> bringing together film by contemporary artists working across Southeast Asian themes and geographies. Through documentary, animation and speculative storytelling, these works traverse disappearing islands, shifting coastlines, local mythologies and</span> maritime journeys<span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">. Together, they offer Southeast Asian interpretations of the Indian Ocean, revealing the histories carried upon its currents.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><em>Animacies of Absence</em>, dir. ila (2025) 21\\\"</p><p><em>The Sea Is a Blue Memory</em>, dir. Priyageetha Dia (2022) 10\\\"</p><p><em>A Spider, A Fever, and Other Disappearing Islands</em>, dir. Natalie Khoo (2022) 21\\\"</p><p><em>Wet Dreams</em> (2024) Jon Cuyson 10\\\"</p><p><br></p><p><strong>All profits from ticket sales will go directly to supporting the work of the ESEA Community Centre in light of recent cuts to its funding!</strong></p><p><br></p><p><a href=\\\"https://www.instagram.com/suyinsays/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" style=\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 242);\\\"><strong>Suyin Haynes</strong></a><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"> is a Malaysian British journalist, lecturer and researcher interested in storytelling at the intersections of identity, culture and underrepresented communities. She’s a lecturer in journalism at City St George’s, University of London, and is completing an MA in Southeast Asian Studies at SOAS.&nbsp;</span></p><p><br></p><p><a href=\\\"https://www.instagram.com/esea.filmnight/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" style=\\\"background-color: transparent; color: rgb(0, 0, 242);\\\"><strong>ESEA Film Night</strong></a><strong style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"> </strong><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">is </span>a volunteer-led monthly film night celebrating East and Southeast Asian cinema.</p><p><br></p><p><a href=\\\"https://www.instagram.com/brixtoncommunitycinema/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" style=\\\"color: rgb(0, 0, 242);\\\"><strong>Brixton Community Cinema</strong></a> is a <span style=\\\"color: rgb(12, 16, 20);\\\">volunteer-run film club bringing affordable screenings to Lambeth to address uneven access to arts institutions.</span></p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"ESEA Community Centre\",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a6a30fe1265a847027b1daf\",\"name\":\"ESEA Community Centre\",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"12-14 Englefield Rd, \\nLondon, \\nN1 4LS, \\nUnited Kingdom\\n\",\"default_capacity\":null,\"color\":\"#ff8fdc\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-10-04T15:00:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-10-04T17:00:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":60,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a6a30f4eeff9c71f4e3cc7e\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":800,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":60,\"quantity_sold\":23,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":37,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":300,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-08-18T16:52:48.715Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785344245202temp-1785344245123-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785344245432temp-1785344245375-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[],\"terms_at_checkout\":\"<p>All profits from ticket sales will go directly to supporting the ESEA Community Centre in light of recent cuts to its funding, please donate what you are able to to support their vital work.</p>\"},{\"id\":\"6a6f299bb99d553cf0143ca7\",\"event_bucket\":\"6a69961accdb8cb546a29d58\",\"timezone\":\"Europe/London\",\"name\":\"The Hour of Liberation Has Arrived + discussion with John Akomfrah and Imran Perretta\",\"description\":\"<p><strong><em>The Hour of Liberations Has Arrived</em> (1974) dir. Heiny Srour 62\\\"</strong></p><p><br></p><p>In 1970 in the Dhofar region of Oman, an extraordinary guerrilla movement rose against the son of the Sultan of Oman who had just been overthrown in a coup organised by the British secret service. Named the Popular Front for the Liberation of Oman and the Arabian Gulf, it momentarily created a secular, feminist and equalitarian society. Made collectively, <em>The Hour of Liberation has Arrived, is </em>the only film to document the brief existence of this radical historic moment, drawing on Arab oral traditions from the testimony of liberation fighters to militant songs and slogans, capturing a revolutionary society in the making.</p><p><br></p><p><span style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\">Included in your ticket are snacks by </span><a href=\\\"https://www.instagram.com/sahanisaanii/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\" style=\\\"background-color: transparent;\\\"><strong>S</strong></a><a href=\\\"https://www.instagram.com/sahanisaanii/\\\" target=\\\"_blank\\\"><strong>ahani Saanii</strong></a>, a non-fixed food research and learning space engaging people in culinary histories of Western Indian and the Swahili Coast through suppers, workshops, and other experimental formats.&nbsp;</p><p><br></p><p>This screening, a finale to Brixton Community Cinema's last season, will be followed by a conversation with filmmakers <strong>John Akomfrah</strong> and <strong>Imran Perretta</strong>, moderated by Brixton Community Cinema founder <strong>Abiba Coulibaly.</strong></p>\",\"short_description\":\"\",\"location\":\"The Cinema Museum \",\"venue\":{\"id\":\"6a6f2983ebf7e6764327bd7e\",\"name\":\"The Cinema Museum \",\"short_name\":\"\",\"description\":\"\",\"address\":\"\",\"default_capacity\":null,\"color\":\"#fffc36\",\"use_reserved_seating\":false,\"comscore_include\":false},\"is_dated\":true,\"start_time\":\"2026-10-06T18:00:00.000Z\",\"end_time\":\"2026-10-06T20:30:00.000Z\",\"cutoff_event_watch_at_availability_end\":false,\"tags\":[],\"visibility\":\"visible\",\"hide_tickets_button\":false,\"require_mailing_address\":false,\"require_phone_number\":false,\"rush_line_enabled\":true,\"passholder_ticket_required\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_enabled\":true,\"standalone_ticket_sales_unlocked\":false,\"sales_disabled_unless_coupon\":false,\"show_custom_checkout_message\":false,\"disable_pass_quick_order\":false,\"credits_disabled\":false,\"tickets_available\":true,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":200,\"ticket_buckets\":[{\"id\":\"6a78c8fb7b7e3a5f3fd2a7c3\",\"name\":\"General Admission\",\"price\":600,\"unlimited\":false,\"quantity\":200,\"quantity_sold\":116,\"quantity_scanned\":0,\"quantity_remaining\":84,\"public\":true,\"autoissue_companion_ticket\":[],\"exclude_capacity\":false,\"lock_admin_only\":false,\"variable_price\":true,\"variable_price_minimum\":100,\"variants\":[],\"applicable_pass_buckets\":[],\"is_active\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null}],\"films\":[],\"updated_at\":\"2026-10-06T18:21:48.676Z\",\"is_virtual\":false,\"comscore_include\":true,\"geographic_restrictions\":[],\"geographic_exclusions\":[],\"vague_geographic_restrictions\":false,\"show_in_coming_soon\":false,\"allow_preorder\":false,\"aarp_member_required\":false,\"show_quantity_countdown\":false,\"virtual_balloting_enabled\":false,\"virtual_balloting_inline_during_playback\":false,\"use_default_event_bucket_preroll\":true,\"virtual_content_preroll\":[],\"virtual_content_preroll_visible\":true,\"use_default_event_bucket_postroll\":true,\"virtual_content_postroll\":[],\"virtual_content_postroll_visible\":true,\"virtual_intervals\":null,\"display_ads\":[],\"images\":{\"cover\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785780206252temp-1785780206217-cover.jpg\",\"avatar\":\"https://static-a.eventive.org/1785780206531temp-1785780206514-avatar.jpg\"},\"ledger_tags\":[],\"rules\":[]}],\"includes_all_events\":true}",
